comments.body:
I broadly agree with this, if I am only interested for example in allowing `Googlebot`, I would as a consumer find it much easier to subscribe to a Service specific file, and not waste bytes and time having to parse out only the relevant ones.

I also imagine it's perhaps easier for publishers to create files that automatically contain a subset of IP ranges filtered to appropriate services, perhaps even determined from URL, e.g. `/ipranges/all/ip-ranges.json` for all published IP ranges, `/ipranges/googlebot/ip-ranges.json`

I also like the addition of a field that could act as a pointer as to where to find out more about the service. I get there is a requirement to publish a link to the jafar file on the crawler description page, I think it would be good to have this file point back.

An alternative might be allow either a string or an array of strings for service if it remains scoped in the prefixes, e.g.

```
{
  "creation_time": "2026-03-16T13:00:00.000Z",
  "synctoken": "....",
  "notes": "This file contains the IP ranges for some services",
  "formatVersion": "1.0",
  "prefixes": [
    {
      "ipv4Prefix": "66.249.78.32/272",
      "service": [
        {
          "name": "Googlebot",
          "explainer": "https://developers.google.com/search/docs/advanced/crawling/verifying-googlebot"
        },
        {
          "name": "Google-Safety",
          "explainer": "https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawlers-fetchers/google-special-case-crawlers"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

That would avoid having to repeat IP addresses for each service.
